{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Mechanism CONFIRMED (AC1), and the fix is authored on branch `fix-caused-by-lagging-checkout`.\n\n**Divergence point, cited.** `commit_impact.referencing_commits` ran\n`git -C <repo_root> log --format=%H%x1f%B%x00` with **no rev argument**\n(`src/rebar/_engine_support/commit_impact.py:186-192`, pre-fix), so it walked HEAD alone.\n`caused_by_refusal` (`src/rebar/_commands/link_revert.py:31`) passes\n`_code_root(repo_root)` = `config.repo_root(None)`, which through MCP is\n`REBAR_ROOT=/var/gerrit/site/mcp-code` — cloned ONCE at container boot by\n`infra/scripts/mcp-entrypoint.sh:290` and never checked out again. The snapshot machinery's\n`fetch_origin` advances `refs/remotes/origin/main`; nothing advances HEAD.\n\n**Reproduced (not inferred).** A `--single-branch` clone, one upstream commit subject\n`d80d-7be7-1c0a-4231: async gate start (P2)`, then `git fetch` in the clone:\n`git log` shows only the clone-time commit, `git log --all` shows both, and\n`referencing_commits` returns `[]` in the clone against `['c67195b…']` in the up-to-date\ncheckout. Same ticket, same commit, two verdicts — the CLI/MCP split in one process.\n\n**Independent corroboration.** `_engine/rebar_reconciler/apply_inbound_records.py:328-338`\nalready forces every inbound link, with a comment naming this exact mechanism: \"its evidence\n(e.g. the introducing commit behind a caused_by) may live only in the peer's clone.\"\n\n**Fix.** The scan gains an opt-in `include_upstream` that adds the remote-tracking refs for\nthe checkout's OWN branch. Deliberately NOT `--all`: rebar's default layout keeps the ticket\nevent log on a `tickets` branch of the SAME repository, so `--all` walks one commit per\nticket event — wrong history, unbounded volume. When that still finds nothing, the guard\nonly treats absence as PROVEN by a checkout that is the whole history: no remote qualifies\noutright; a replica is refreshed once (bounded `git fetch` under the existing\n`fetch_coordination_lock`) and rescanned; a refresh that cannot complete leaves absence\nunestablished, which is the same epistemic position as an unreadable history and takes the\nsame answer — allow. The check is NOT skipped on MCP, and the refusal is unchanged for a\ngenuinely commit-less target in a complete checkout.\n\nThe close gate's scan keeps its exact argv (`include_upstream` is opt-in), so its\n\"reachable from your worktree's current HEAD\" contract is untouched — pinned by a test.\n\n**Tests (RED before GREEN).** Four new behavioural tests in\n`tests/unit/test_caused_by_link_validation.py` all failed with the reported refusal text\nbefore the fix and pass after; one of them drives the **MCP `link_tickets` tool** with\n`REBAR_ROOT` pointed at the lagging checkout, so the defect's own entry point is proven\nrather than the library's. A fifth pins the close gate's HEAD-scoped scan against widening."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788660328249516001,"uuid":"74cc87f9-edb5-4d69-8994-941f3f8847f5"}